1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
I am Daniel Desjardins and I am a Scoutmaster with through 426 in London Dairy, New Hampshire.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:11,000
This edition of the Scoutmaster Podcast is sponsored by backers like me

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,000
And now the old Scoutmaster.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Hey everybody, this is Clark Green. It's summertime and so our next few podcasts feature on-court presentations

5
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,000
from our archives, And I want to replay an interview that I thought I would never get,

6
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
that I did a while back with Survivorman Les Stroud.

7
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:56,000
There are reality shows and reality shows, and I always thought that Survivorman was the best of its genre.

8
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,000
My interview with Les Stroud came right after he published the book called The Will to Live,

9
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:09,000
just with some really vivid survival stories and a lot of very practical real-world survival logic.

10
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:17,000
As always, this podcast is only possible because of the generosity of the folks who become ScoutmasterCGcom backers.

11
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,000
Go to ScoutmasterCGcom, click the support link at the top of the page.

12
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
You'll find out how you can become a ScoutmasterCGcom backer.

13
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:31,000
We'll be back with our regular schedule of podcasts in a few weeks. Until then, let's get started, shall we?

14
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Today I'm speaking with Survivorman Les Stroud about his book Will to Live. Welcome to the Scoutmaster Podcast, Les.

15
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:39,000
How are you?

16
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,000
I'm doing real well. How are things up there in Huntsville?

17
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Very well, very well. good,

18
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:46,000
You grew up in Toronto, if I have it right,

19
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,000
That's correct. yeah, just on the wet end of Toronto. Mimico,

20
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Did you do any scouting when you were growing up?

21
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Just a very little bit when I was younger. yes, I did. There was an active Scout troop in the area.

22
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:06,000
I think at some point it might have dissolved at some point, but yes, when I was young I was out for sure.

23
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:10,000
My sister also did Girlguides.

24
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Then you ended up somewhere along the line in Tomagami in a canoe.

25
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Yeah, well, for many years I grew up as a big fan of the wilderness and nature and all of that stuff,

26
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Whatever the TV would offer me. but I would go to my cottage and definitely I would make little shelters

27
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:40,000
and I would get out in the back 40 and sort of just play around and do everything I had to out in the back.

28
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:45,000
I just loved it, adored it. but I also got away from it as I got a little bit older and got into music.

29
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,000
Then I got back into it again when I discovered canoe tripping up in Tomagami.

30
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
It's a fantastic area. It's like the jewel of the north.

31
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:58,000
I love Algonquin Park, but Tomagami is kind of like Algonquin Park with no people. so it's beautiful.

32
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,000
We bring a crew of boys up and do a week in Algonquin every summer.

33
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:03,000
It's beautiful.

34
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:09,000
So your trip to Tomagami, that was kind of a turning point and you ended up becoming a canoe guide.

35
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Yeah, it was after many years of being a musician and working in film and all of that sort of stuff

36
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:19,000
that I finally got invited at some point on a canoe trip with some friends.

37
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:25,000
I just absolutely loved it. It was so fantastic and so beautiful in Tomagami to do it.

38
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Once I got that taste of that sort of outdoor adventure life for real- and I bear in mind I was older now.

39
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:35,000
I never looked back.

40
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Yeah, and that led to you setting up a production company that put together a couple of survivor specials

41
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:45,000
for the Canadian Discovery Channel.

42
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Yeah, what it was was I always wanted to do a home DVD series, which actually back then would have been

43
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:58,000
a home VHS and video series on survival, but at one point I realized that the time was right

44
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:03,000
for me to pitch it to the television networks and so I did that instead of just producing it on my own.

45
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:06,000
and, lo and behold, they loved the idea.

46
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:14,000
So the very first survivor man sort of thing that I did was- I call it well since the two pilot versions,

47
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:23,000
I call them Stranded, and it was for Discovery Channel Canada at the time and I just love doing it

48
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:29,000
and it went over so so well that I then took it upon myself to pitch it as a full-on series called Survivor Man.

49
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:33,000
That's where most of the people who are listening are going to know you from, from that series.

50
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,000
It's just a great series, one of my favorites,

51
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:41,000
Thank you, yes, Well, I don't think people realize that I did it for nearly nine years

52
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:45,000
and then moved on to do Beyond Survival, which is the next series

53
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:50,000
and it's playing right now constantly, which was like Survivor Man, all grown up

54
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:54,000
because it was out surviving with the indigenous peoples around the world.

55
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,000
So it's been over ten years that I've been doing it.

56
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Yeah, the one thing I really love about this show is how honest it is

57
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:06,000
and how nothing is really romanticized.

58
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:11,000
The thing I think about are the scenes where you're up at night in a shelter somewhere

59
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:17,000
with that kind of night vision, green lighting, and you're talking about, you know,

60
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:21,000
being afraid or being kind of uncertain about what's going to happen next.

61
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,000
I've actually been in a situation like that,

62
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:30,000
and I don't know how you can do a better job of explaining what the whole thing feels like.

63
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:38,000
Well, I think you know the whole deal was just to really truly go through stuff.

64
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:43,000
I mean, I obviously did hundreds of survival experiences before I started filming them,

65
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:50,000
and so this was a matter of just going through the way. I always let the real things happen,

66
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:54,000
let the chips fall as they may, pull no punches, fumble and bumble like anybody else would

67
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:57,000
have some successes, like anybody else would.

68
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:01,000
and then, as I film it, you know, try to capture it in a way that is captivating,

69
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
but at the same time, just tell the story as it is

70
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:08,000
and don't try to trump up the drama. just show it as it is,

71
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,000
because I think being lost in the wilderness is drama enough

72
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:14,000
And it's not fun. I mean, it's not

73
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:17,000
No, it's never fun. I think that's the big thing too, is people. you know

74
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:21,000
it's become a recreational activity. survival has

75
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:26,000
It's become that, and partially through shows like my own.

76
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:31,000
but survival in its true essence is nothing recreational about it.

77
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:34,000
It's miserable, It's uncomfortable, It's cold

78
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,000
And in reality, you don't want to build a shelter,

79
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,000
You don't want to build traps, You don't want to make this or that.

80
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,000
You don't want to be, you know, MacGyvering items into other items.

81
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
What you want is to go home.

82
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,000
The key to survival is getting out and going home.

83
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:52,000
That is the whole point of survival is going home.

84
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,000
Once it becomes a sort of recreational side of things,

85
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,000
well, that's fun because the skills are really enjoyable

86
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,000
and that's how you hone your survival abilities.

87
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,000
But in truth you really just want to go home

88
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:07,000
And that kind of puts us right into the book Will to Live.

89
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:10,000
I've read a number of books about survival and things like that,

90
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:13,000
but I think this is really unique because it's kind of like three books in one:

91
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:19,000
You have the narrative and then you have the analysis of what goes on

92
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:22,000
and then you have a lot of practical advice kind of woven in.

93
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:25,000
Yeah, well, I mean I wanted to keep the book very alive and flowing

94
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:28,000
And I also know that there have been a number of books out there

95
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:30,000
that discuss different survival stories,

96
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,000
but they always come from the angle of the psychology.

97
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:35,000
It's like, oh, the psychology, the psychology, the will to live, the mental,

98
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:39,000
And I'm like saying, well, yeah, okay, I get it.

99
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,000
But what about if survival is 90% in the mind?

100
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:44,000
what about that last 10%,

101
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
Because that last 10% is the stuff that you know

102
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:51,000
is how do you get from A to B and what are the right decisions to make,

103
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,000
and what do you eat and don't eat,

104
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:56,000
And those things can be ridiculously fascinating.

105
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:59,000
And it was my chance to take a look at some of these stories

106
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:01,000
And then, after my years of experience of doing it,

107
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:05,000
also inject my opinion and say, well, you know, that was a dumb move,

108
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,000
You know, this person should never have done that,

109
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:10,000
No matter what way you slice it. I get it

110
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:14,000
And I wasn't there, but generally speaking, that was a dumb move.

111
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:16,000
Or you know what? I wasn't there,

112
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:17,000
None of us were there.

113
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
This would not seem like a good move,

114
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:22,000
but it's pretty hard to judge because we were not there.

115
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:26,000
And then, conversely, finally would be: that was a brilliant move.

116
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:29,000
You know, I have no idea how he had the gumption to get up and do that,

117
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:33,000
but you know, thinking to do that certain thing saved his life,

118
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:36,000
And the stories run the gamut of that in many other ways.

119
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:38,000
That's what I think is really the strength of the book.

120
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:43,000
You get a really good picture of what you call the survivor's mindset.

121
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,000
What is that mindset.

122
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:55,000
Well, the bottom line is remembering that survival is, for all intent and purposes

123
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:58,000
it's a verb, you know it's proactive.

124
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:00,000
There is nothing passive about survival,

125
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,000
and that's something that I always try to get across.

126
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,000
You cannot be passive,

127
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:09,000
You cannot be mild and meek and gentle and flow with the energy that flow.

128
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:12,000
No, you're starving and you have to go home

129
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:14,000
and you have to scale a wall to do it.

130
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:19,000
Survival is a practical, sometimes aggressive activity

131
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:22,000
And you want to be active in your own survival,

132
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:26,000
Because if you're going to be passive and let things come what may,

133
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,000
then guess what you're going to get.

134
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:30,000
You're going to get things come what may

135
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,000
And they could be bad things.

136
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:38,000
So that mindset is the mindset of being strong in your mind to know

137
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:43,000
it's the same mindset as the person who gets up off the couch.

138
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,000
You know those who sit on the couch and don't get up

139
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
and those of us who get up off the couch,

140
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,000
And that's what life is about.

141
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,000
Life is about getting up off the couch and doing,

142
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:59,000
And survival is perhaps an extreme example of that,

143
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:03,000
where, if you don't get up and do something, you very well may die.

144
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:10,000
So the mindset is one for survival that is very proactive and self-preservative.

145
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:15,000
You talk about additive factors in survival

146
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
And you kind of analyze those

147
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:20,000
Factors like the will to live, like luck and things like that.

148
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:25,000
Well, I think I was really glad in Will to Live to take the end of each chapter

149
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:28,000
And it's a bit of a device,

150
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:30,000
It's a bit of a literary device for me to say,

151
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
okay, I'm going to actually grade these guys

152
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:36,000
And it's a bit of a guess.

153
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:37,000
But looking at their stories,

154
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:40,000
you can look at something like the Robertsons in their life raft

155
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:41,000
floating in the ocean.

156
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:44,000
Well, for goodness sakes, they had turtles bump into their raft

157
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:46,000
nearly every single day, providing them with ample food,

158
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
and rainstorms every time they needed it,

159
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,000
providing them with ample water.

160
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:53,000
And that's that additive force that we call luck.

161
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,000
I was like, wow, that's pretty lucky.

162
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:59,000
And then Yossi Ginsburg: in terms of will to live,

163
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:03,000
everything happened to this guy in the jungle

164
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:06,000
Just when you couldn't think it could get worse

165
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:07,000
it would,

166
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,000
And yet he kept on surviving

167
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:12,000
And his will was. his luck was terrible,

168
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:15,000
But his will to keep going was supreme.

169
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:18,000
So you know, there are so many variables

170
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:24,000
in the added forces of what it takes to survive,

171
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:27,000
Like what kind of kit do you have with you,

172
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
And what kind of knowledge base do you have?

173
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
The guys up on the top of the mountain in the Andes?

174
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:34,000
the Uruguayan rugby team that had the resort to cannibalism

175
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:35,000
had no survival skills.

176
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
Some of these kids had never even been out of the city.

177
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
So zero survival skills.

178
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:43,000
And that's where Douglas Lawson, in his trek across the Antarctic,

179
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:46,000
was the most supreme Arctic survivor ever.

180
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,000
So his skills were supreme,

181
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:51,000
Yet both of them found themselves in near-death experiences.

182
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:55,000
So it's really interesting to see how different individuals

183
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:59,000
play out, given their circumstances

184
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:02,000
and depending on what additive force they're strong in.

185
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:03,000
Do they have a lot of luck?

186
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
Do they have a great survival kit?

187
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
Do they have an incredible amount of knowledge,

188
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
Or do they have a supreme will to live?

189
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
Those are the four top ones.

190
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:14,000
After that there's another 20, you know variables,

191
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:20,000
And I guess the way of guaranteeing or preparing yourself

192
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:23,000
is to try and make sure that all those additive factors

193
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
are on your side

194
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:28,000
As much as you can, and that's preparation. right

195
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
Preparation is the key. there

196
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
You can get the knowledge ahead of time,

197
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:36,000
You can have a well-stocked survival kit,

198
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:42,000
You can bolster your will to live even by having this knowledge,

199
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:45,000
having the knowledge of where you're going where this escapes.

200
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
So where's the escape route?

201
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:51,000
You're going out with a group and you've got a guide.

202
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:54,000
Well, ask the guide if you can see the map,

203
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:57,000
Get him to show you where's the closest roads,

204
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:58,000
where's the escape route.

205
00:12:58,000 --> 00:12:59,000
So if you get halfway down the river

206
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
and the guide himself has an appendicitis attack,

207
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:04,000
you might be the only one who knows that there's a highway

208
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:05,000
two miles to the east.

209
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:10,000
And when you prepare yourself and bolster your will to live

210
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:13,000
by getting that knowledge from the guides themselves,

211
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:16,000
you become an asset, not a liability,

212
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:19,000
And then you don't have to worry about the bad luck so much,

213
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:22,000
because these other three angles you can build up,

214
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:26,000
And that again is being proactive, about the situation,

215
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
not just letting things happen to you,

216
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:29,000
but making things happen.

217
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:30,000
Yeah, absolutely.

218
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:34,000
What do you think is the most important way

219
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:37,000
for people who are going to take a group of young people

220
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
into the wilderness to prepare themselves

221
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
for those eventualities?

222
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:46,000
I think the most important thing is the sharing of the knowledge.

223
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,000
We should all know what we all know.

224
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:53,000
You know you don't want just the leader to know

225
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:57,000
what the escape routes are. where the first aid kit is,

226
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
what's in the first aid kit, how to use it

227
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:00,000
where the survival kit is.

228
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,000
You don't want just. that's the way it happens,

229
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:04,000
especially with younger people too,

230
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:07,000
because younger people get out and they don't want to ask.

231
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:08,000
You know what I'm afraid to ask.

232
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:09,000
Well, they should ask:

233
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:10,000
You're going out into the wilderness,

234
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
